   "points": [
    "Learn el or la with every noun; -o and -a are a guide, not a rule.",
    "The verb ending says who's acting, so the subject pronoun is usually dropped.",
    "ser for what something is; estar for how and where it is.",
    "gustar agrees with the thing you like: me gusta el libro, me gustan los libros.",
    "Object pronouns go before a conjugated verb and attach to infinitives, gerunds and positive commands.",
    "ir a + infinitive is the everyday future.",
    "Indefinido tells what happened; imperfecto paints the background.",
    "por looks back at a cause or a route; para looks forward to a purpose or destination.",
    "The subjunctive follows que after wishes, feelings and doubt, and cuando about the future.",
    "Double negatives are correct in Spanish (no … nada, no … nadie)."
   ],
   "tip": "Listening strategy: catch the verb ending first: it tells you who and when. Then listen for the small words before the verb (lo, le, se, me): they tell you to whom."
